-- Migration: Bloom's L3 rebalancing — REG batch 1 (45 questions)
-- Date: 2026-02-21
-- Purpose: Rewrite 45 L2 (Application) questions to L3 (Analysis) depth for REG section
-- Affected topics: Basis, Individual Income, C Corps, Partnerships, Gains/Losses, 1031 Exchanges, S Corps, Estate/Gift

BEGIN;

-- ============================================================
-- PROPERTY TRANSACTIONS: BASIS (8 questions)
-- ============================================================

-- ID 70: Property Transactions: Basis — Gift basis analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Nakamura receives a painting as a gift from her uncle. The uncle''s adjusted basis is $30,000 and the fair market value at the date of the gift is $50,000. No gift tax was paid. Six months later, Nakamura sells the painting for $45,000. How does the gift basis rule affect the amount of gain Nakamura must recognize, and why?',
  choices = '["$15,000 gain, because the basis steps up to FMV at the date of the gift and gain is measured from $30,000", "$15,000 gain, because the carryover basis from the donor preserves the donor''s unrealized appreciation in the recipient''s hands", "$0 gain, because no gift tax was paid and the recipient takes a zero basis", "$45,000 gain, because gift property always takes a basis of $0 to prevent tax avoidance"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Under the gift basis rules, when FMV exceeds the donor''s adjusted basis at the time of the gift, the recipient carries over the donor''s basis ($30,000). This preserves the donor''s $20,000 of unrealized appreciation, which is recognized when Nakamura sells for $45,000 ($45,000 − $30,000 = $15,000 gain). (A) is wrong because the basis does not step up to FMV for gifts — that rule applies to inherited property under Section 1014. (C) is wrong because the absence of gift tax affects only a potential basis adjustment, not whether gain exists; carryover basis still applies. (D) is wrong because gift property never takes a zero basis — the carryover rule under Section 1015 specifically prevents this result.'
WHERE id = 70;

-- ID 71: Property Transactions: Basis — Inherited vs. gift basis comparison
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Priya''s grandmother purchased stock for $40,000 many years ago. The stock''s fair market value is $95,000 when the grandmother dies on June 15, 2025, and Priya inherits it. If the grandmother had instead gifted the stock to Priya one month before death, how would the basis difference between the gift and inheritance affect Priya''s taxable gain on an immediate sale at $95,000?',
  choices = '["No difference — both gift and inheritance would produce the same $55,000 gain", "The inheritance produces $0 gain because the stepped-up basis equals FMV, while the gift would produce $55,000 gain because the carryover basis preserves the donor''s original cost", "The gift produces $0 gain because gifts are tax-free, while the inheritance produces $55,000 gain", "Both produce $0 gain because transfers between family members are always nontaxable"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Under Section 1014, inherited property receives a stepped-up basis to FMV at the date of death ($95,000), so selling immediately at $95,000 produces $0 gain. Under Section 1015, gift property carries over the donor''s basis ($40,000), so selling at $95,000 produces a $55,000 gain. This $55,000 difference illustrates why the stepped-up basis at death is one of the most significant tax benefits in the Code. (A) is wrong because the two transfer methods produce fundamentally different basis outcomes. (C) reverses the analysis — gifts are excluded from income to the recipient but do not eliminate gain on subsequent sale. (D) is wrong because the tax treatment depends on the transfer method (gift vs. inheritance), not the family relationship.'
WHERE id = 71;

-- ID 212: Property Transactions: Basis — Land vs. building allocation impact
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Kowalski purchases a rental property for $250,000, with $50,000 allocated to land and $200,000 to the building. His neighbor, Park, purchases a nearly identical property for $250,000 but allocates $100,000 to land and $150,000 to the building based on a different appraisal. How does the allocation difference affect the two investors'' annual depreciation deductions and why?',
  choices = '["No effect — both properties cost $250,000 so both produce the same depreciation deduction", "Park gets larger depreciation deductions because a higher land allocation increases the depreciable basis", "Kowalski gets smaller depreciation deductions because the IRS requires equal allocation between land and building", "Kowalski gets larger annual depreciation deductions because only the building portion is depreciable, and his building allocation ($200,000) exceeds Park''s ($150,000)"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (D): Land is never depreciable because it does not have a determinable useful life. Only the building allocation creates a depreciable basis. Kowalski''s $200,000 building allocation produces larger annual depreciation deductions than Park''s $150,000 building allocation, even though both paid $250,000 total. Over 27.5 years, Kowalski deducts approximately $7,273/year while Park deducts approximately $5,455/year — a $1,818 annual difference stemming entirely from allocation. (A) is wrong because total cost is irrelevant — only the depreciable portion matters. (B) is wrong because it reverses the relationship; higher land allocation reduces the depreciable base. (C) is wrong because there is no equal-allocation requirement; the split must reflect fair market values supported by appraisal.'
WHERE id = 212;

-- ID 213: Property Transactions: Basis — Personal-to-rental conversion analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Foster purchased a personal residence for $300,000. When the local market declined, the home''s FMV dropped to $260,000, and Foster converted it to rental property. Foster''s CPA explains that the depreciation basis will be $260,000 rather than $300,000. What is the effect of the lower-of-cost-or-FMV rule on Foster, and why does the tax code use this approach for personal-to-rental conversions?',
  choices = '["The rule benefits Foster by creating a built-in loss that can be recognized immediately upon conversion", "The rule uses the lower of cost or FMV ($260,000) to prevent taxpayers from generating depreciation deductions based on personal-use losses that were economically incurred while the property served a non-deductible purpose", "The rule has no practical effect because Foster can elect to use original cost as the depreciation basis", "The rule uses FMV only to match the property''s current market position, regardless of whether it is higher or lower than cost"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): When a personal residence is converted to rental property, the basis for depreciation is the lower of the taxpayer''s adjusted basis ($300,000) or FMV at conversion ($260,000). The policy rationale is that the $40,000 decline occurred while the property was personal-use — a period when losses are nondeductible. Allowing depreciation on the full $300,000 would effectively let the taxpayer recover a personal loss through business deductions, which the Code prevents. (A) is wrong because the rule prevents loss recognition, not enables it — no immediate deduction arises from conversion. (C) is wrong because there is no election; the lower-of rule is mandatory. (D) is wrong because FMV is not always used — if FMV exceeded cost, the original cost would be the depreciation basis, not FMV.'
WHERE id = 213;

-- ID 214: Property Transactions: Basis — Taxable vs. nontaxable exchange basis comparison
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Rivera exchanges property with an adjusted basis of $70,000 and FMV of $85,000, plus $15,000 cash, for property with an FMV of $100,000 in a fully taxable exchange. Her colleague, Okafor, completes an identical transaction but qualifies for Section 1031 like-kind exchange treatment. How does the basis of the property received differ between the two transactions, and what causes this difference?',
  choices = '["Rivera takes a $100,000 FMV basis because taxable exchanges set basis at fair market value, reflecting that all gain or loss is recognized at the time of exchange; Okafor takes a carryover basis because Section 1031 defers gain recognition", "Both take a $70,000 carryover basis because the basis of property given up always carries over regardless of taxability", "Rivera takes a $85,000 basis (FMV of property given up) while Okafor takes a $70,000 basis, because taxable exchanges use the FMV surrendered", "Both take a $100,000 basis because the FMV of property received always determines basis in any exchange"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): In a taxable exchange, the taxpayer recognizes all gain or loss at the time of the transaction, so the basis of the property received equals its FMV ($100,000) — the taxpayer gets a fresh start. In a Section 1031 exchange, gain is deferred, so the basis carries over from the relinquished property (adjusted for boot paid), preserving the unrecognized gain in the replacement property''s lower basis. This difference illustrates the core principle: basis reflects the amount of previously taxed investment. (B) is wrong because carryover basis applies only to nonrecognition transactions, not taxable exchanges. (C) is wrong because taxable exchange basis is set by FMV of property received, not FMV of property given up. (D) is wrong because Section 1031 specifically prevents using FMV as the new basis — that would eliminate the deferred gain.'
WHERE id = 214;

-- ID 511: Property Transactions: Basis — Section 179 phase-out impact analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Hartley Manufacturing places $3,200,000 of qualifying equipment in service during 2025. The Section 179 limit is $1,220,000 with a phase-out threshold at $3,050,000. A competitor, Greystone Industries, places $3,000,000 of identical equipment in service. What is the effect of the $150,000 difference in equipment spending on each company''s maximum Section 179 deduction, and why does this phase-out mechanism exist?',
  choices = '["Both companies receive the full $1,220,000 deduction because the phase-out only applies above $4,000,000", "Hartley receives $1,070,000 while Greystone receives the full $1,220,000, because the dollar-for-dollar phase-out reduces Hartley''s deduction by the $150,000 excess over the threshold — a mechanism designed to target the benefit toward smaller businesses", "Hartley receives $0 while Greystone receives $1,220,000, because exceeding the threshold by any amount eliminates the entire deduction", "Hartley receives $3,200,000 while Greystone receives $3,000,000, because Section 179 allows a full deduction for all qualifying property regardless of limits"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Section 179 uses a dollar-for-dollar phase-out: for every dollar of qualifying property placed in service above the $3,050,000 threshold, the maximum deduction is reduced by $1. Hartley exceeds the threshold by $150,000 ($3,200,000 − $3,050,000), reducing its maximum from $1,220,000 to $1,070,000. Greystone stays below the threshold and claims the full $1,220,000. The phase-out''s policy purpose is to target the incentive toward small and mid-sized businesses — once spending reaches high levels, Congress assumes the taxpayer can afford to depreciate assets over their useful lives. (A) is wrong because the phase-out begins at $3,050,000, not $4,000,000. (C) is wrong because the phase-out is gradual, not a cliff; exceeding the threshold reduces the deduction dollar-for-dollar rather than eliminating it. (D) is wrong because Section 179 is subject to both a dollar limit and a phase-out — it does not allow unlimited deductions.'
WHERE id = 511;

-- ID 513: Property Transactions: Basis — Listed property business-use threshold analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Thompson uses a $60,000 automobile 70% for business. Her associate, Valdez, uses an identical $60,000 automobile 45% for business. Both vehicles qualify as listed property. How does the difference in business-use percentage affect the depreciation method each taxpayer must use, and what is the consequence if Thompson''s business use drops below 50% in a subsequent year?',
  choices = '["Both use MACRS accelerated depreciation regardless of business-use percentage because the listed property rules were repealed by the TCJA", "Thompson uses straight-line depreciation on 70% while Valdez uses MACRS accelerated on 45%, because lower business use qualifies for more favorable treatment", "No difference — both are limited to the luxury auto caps and neither can claim any depreciation until business use exceeds 80%", "Thompson qualifies for MACRS accelerated depreciation on the 70% business portion (subject to luxury limits), while Valdez must use straight-line because business use is 50% or below; if Thompson''s use later drops below 50%, she must recapture the excess depreciation previously claimed"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (D): The listed property rules under Section 280F create a critical threshold at 50% business use. Thompson''s 70% use exceeds the threshold, qualifying her for MACRS accelerated depreciation on the business portion (subject to luxury auto dollar limits). Valdez''s 45% use falls at or below 50%, restricting her to straight-line depreciation on the business portion. The recapture provision is the analytical key: if Thompson''s business use later drops to 50% or below, she must recapture the difference between accelerated depreciation previously claimed and what straight-line would have allowed. This prevents taxpayers from claiming accelerated deductions and then shifting to personal use. (A) is wrong because the TCJA did not repeal listed property rules for automobiles. (B) reverses the treatment — higher business use gets the more favorable method. (C) is wrong because the threshold is >50%, not 80%, and both taxpayers can claim some depreciation.'
WHERE id = 513;

-- ID 514: Property Transactions: Basis — TCJA bonus depreciation phase-down analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Meridian Corp. purchases $500,000 of new manufacturing equipment in 2025, while its subsidiary, Atlas Corp., purchases identical equipment in 2026. Under the TCJA bonus depreciation phase-down schedule, how does the one-year timing difference affect each company''s first-year bonus depreciation deduction, and what trend does this illustrate?',
  choices = '["Meridian deducts $200,000 (40%) in 2025 while Atlas deducts $100,000 (20%) in 2026, illustrating the TCJA''s planned annual reduction in bonus depreciation that phases the benefit to zero by 2027", "Both deduct $500,000 (100%) because the TCJA made bonus depreciation permanent at 100%", "Meridian deducts $100,000 (20%) while Atlas deducts $200,000 (40%) because bonus rates increase over time to encourage delayed investment", "Meridian deducts $300,000 (60%) while Atlas deducts $250,000 (50%) because the phase-down uses 10% annual increments starting from 60%"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): The TCJA set bonus depreciation at 100% for assets placed in service from September 28, 2017 through 2022, then phases down by 20 percentage points annually: 80% in 2023, 60% in 2024, 40% in 2025, 20% in 2026, and 0% in 2027. Meridian''s 2025 purchase qualifies for 40% ($200,000), while Atlas''s 2026 purchase qualifies for only 20% ($100,000). This declining schedule illustrates Congress''s intent to provide a temporary economic stimulus that gradually returns to regular depreciation rules. (B) is wrong because 100% bonus depreciation was never permanent — the TCJA explicitly included the phase-down schedule. (C) reverses the direction; bonus rates decrease over time, not increase. (D) is wrong about both the starting percentage and the increment size — the phase-down uses 20-point annual reductions starting from 100%.'
WHERE id = 514;

