-- ID 77: Individual Taxation: Income — Constructive receipt analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'On December 28, 2025, Brennan receives a $10,000 signing bonus check from a new employer. Brennan deposits the check on January 3, 2026. Brennan''s coworker, Sato, is promised the same bonus but is told it will not be available until January 5, 2026. How does the constructive receipt doctrine affect the timing of income recognition differently for Brennan and Sato, and why?',
  choices = '["Brennan reports in 2025 because the check was received and available without substantial limitation, while Sato reports in 2026 because the income was subject to a genuine restriction — constructive receipt requires actual or unrestricted access, not a mere promise of future payment", "Both report in 2026 because income is recognized only when cash is deposited, regardless of when it becomes available", "Both report in 2025 because the employer''s obligation arose in 2025 for both employees", "Brennan reports in 2026 (when deposited) while Sato reports in 2025 (when promised), because constructive receipt applies to promises rather than physical possession"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): Constructive receipt applies when income is credited, set apart, or made available to the taxpayer without substantial limitation. Brennan had unrestricted access to the check on December 28, 2025 — the voluntary delay in depositing does not shift income to 2026. Sato, however, faced a genuine restriction: the funds were not available until January 2026, so no constructive receipt occurred in 2025. The distinction turns on whether the taxpayer had the power to receive the income, not whether they chose to exercise it. (B) is wrong because the cash method recognizes income upon receipt or constructive receipt, not upon deposit. (C) is wrong because the employer''s obligation alone does not create constructive receipt — the employee must have actual access. (D) reverses both results and misapplies the doctrine.'
WHERE id = 77;

-- ID 540: Individual Taxation: Income — Rental loss allowance interaction with MAGI
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Nguyen, who is NOT a real estate professional, actively manages a rental property that produces $40,000 in losses. Nguyen''s modified AGI is $90,000. Her neighbor, Petrov, has identical rental losses of $40,000 but modified AGI of $125,000. How does the $25,000 rental loss allowance treat each taxpayer differently, and what causes the difference?',
  choices = '["Both deduct $0 because the passive activity loss rules prohibit all rental loss deductions for non-real-estate professionals", "Nguyen deducts $15,000 while Petrov deducts $25,000, because the allowance increases with higher MAGI to offset the greater tax burden", "Nguyen deducts $40,000 while Petrov deducts $25,000, because taxpayers below $100,000 MAGI face no limitation on rental losses", "Nguyen deducts the full $25,000 allowance with no phase-out, while Petrov deducts only $12,500 because the allowance phases out by $1 for every $2 of MAGI above $100,000 — meaning Petrov''s $25,000 excess MAGI reduces the allowance by $12,500"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (D): The $25,000 rental real estate loss allowance under Section 469(i) applies to active participants with MAGI at or below $100,000 with no reduction. Above $100,000, the allowance phases out at $1 for every $2 of excess MAGI, disappearing entirely at $150,000. Nguyen''s $90,000 MAGI is below the phase-out threshold, so she claims the full $25,000 (limited to the allowance, not the full $40,000 loss). Petrov''s $125,000 MAGI exceeds the threshold by $25,000, reducing the allowance by $12,500 ($25,000 × 50%) to $12,500. (A) is wrong because Section 469(i) creates a specific exception to passive activity rules for active rental participants. (B) reverses the phase-out direction — higher MAGI reduces the benefit. (C) is wrong because the $25,000 cap applies regardless of MAGI level; below $100,000 the cap itself is the limitation.'
WHERE id = 540;

-- ID 544: Individual Taxation: Income — Pass-through income character analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Chen is a 50% partner in a partnership that reports $50,000 of ordinary business income and $10,000 of tax-exempt interest on Schedule K-1. Chen''s partner, Wallace, asks why the tax-exempt interest is reported on the K-1 if it isn''t taxable. What is the effect of including tax-exempt interest on the K-1, and how does it impact Chen''s tax situation differently than the ordinary income?',
  choices = '["Both amounts increase Chen''s gross income equally because all K-1 items are taxable to partners regardless of their character at the partnership level", "The $50,000 ordinary income is excluded from gross income while the $10,000 tax-exempt interest is taxable, because partnership income loses its character when passed through", "The tax-exempt interest is reported to increase Chen''s basis in the partnership but does not increase gross income, while the $50,000 ordinary income is fully includable — the K-1 reports both because basis tracking requires all economic items regardless of taxability", "Neither amount affects Chen because partnership income is taxed only at the entity level"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (C): Under the pass-through rules, all items retain their character from the partnership level to the partner level. The $50,000 ordinary business income is included in Chen''s gross income and is taxable. The $10,000 tax-exempt interest retains its tax-exempt character and is excluded from gross income — but it is still reported on the K-1 because it increases Chen''s outside basis in the partnership (affecting gain/loss calculations on future distributions or sale of the partnership interest). This dual role of K-1 reporting serves both income tax and basis tracking functions. (A) is wrong because tax-exempt income retains its exempt character at the partner level. (B) reverses the treatment of both items. (D) is wrong because partnerships are pass-through entities — income is taxed at the partner level, not the entity level.'
WHERE id = 544;

-- ID 545: Individual Taxation: Income — Passive loss limitation interaction analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Morrison has $30,000 of passive income from Activity A, $50,000 of passive losses from Activity B, and $200,000 in wages. Morrison asks whether the $200,000 in wages can absorb the excess $20,000 of passive losses. What is the effect of the passive activity loss rules on Morrison''s ability to use these losses, and what would change if Morrison disposed of Activity B entirely?',
  choices = '["Morrison can deduct the full $50,000 because passive losses can offset any type of income when the taxpayer has sufficient wage income", "Morrison can deduct $0 because having any passive income disqualifies the taxpayer from using passive losses", "Morrison can deduct $25,000 against wages under the rental real estate allowance, with the remaining $25,000 suspended", "Morrison can deduct only $30,000 (offsetting passive income) with $20,000 suspended; however, if Morrison fully disposes of Activity B in a taxable transaction, all suspended losses from that activity are released and deductible against any income type, including wages"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (D): Under Section 469, passive losses can only offset passive income in the current year. Morrison''s $30,000 of passive income from Activity A absorbs $30,000 of the $50,000 passive losses, leaving $20,000 suspended — wages cannot absorb the excess. However, the disposition rule is the critical analytical distinction: upon a fully taxable disposition of a passive activity, all suspended losses from that activity are released and become deductible against any income, including wages. This ensures that true economic losses are eventually recognized. (A) is wrong because passive losses cannot offset nonpassive income (wages, portfolio income) in the current year. (B) is wrong because having passive income is beneficial — it provides a source to absorb passive losses. (C) is wrong because the $25,000 rental allowance applies only to active participation in rental real estate, not general passive activities.'
WHERE id = 545;

-- ID 547: Individual Taxation: Income — S corp four-tier loss limitation analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Delgado receives a K-1 from an S corporation showing a $40,000 ordinary business loss. Delgado''s stock basis is $25,000 and she has a $10,000 direct loan to the corporation. Her fellow shareholder, Reeves, has the same $40,000 loss allocation but only $25,000 in stock basis and no direct loan — instead, Reeves personally guaranteed a $10,000 corporate bank loan. How does the basis limitation treat these two shareholders differently at the first tier, and why?',
  choices = '["Both deduct $35,000 because personal guarantees and direct loans provide identical basis for loss deduction purposes", "Delgado deducts $10,000 and Reeves deducts $25,000 because loan basis is deducted first and stock basis is only used for the excess", "Delgado deducts the full $40,000 while Reeves deducts $0 because any loan involvement disqualifies the shareholder from loss deductions", "Delgado deducts $35,000 (stock basis $25,000 + direct loan $10,000) while Reeves deducts only $25,000 (stock basis alone), because S corporation debt basis requires a direct economic outlay by the shareholder — personal guarantees of third-party loans do not create basis"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (D): The first tier of the S corporation loss limitation compares the shareholder''s loss allocation to their stock basis plus debt basis from direct loans to the corporation. Delgado has $35,000 of combined basis ($25,000 stock + $10,000 direct loan), allowing $35,000 of the $40,000 loss through the first tier. Reeves has only $25,000 of basis because a personal guarantee of a corporate bank loan does not create shareholder debt basis in an S corporation — the shareholder must make a direct economic outlay to the corporation. This distinguishes S corporations from partnerships, where certain liability allocations can increase basis. The remaining $5,000 (Delgado) and $15,000 (Reeves) are suspended until basis is restored. (A) is wrong because guarantees and direct loans have fundamentally different basis consequences in S corporations. (B) is wrong about the ordering — stock and debt basis are combined, not applied sequentially. (C) is wrong because loan involvement does not disqualify loss deductions; it increases available basis.'
WHERE id = 547;

-- ID 549: Individual Taxation: Income — Limited partner material participation analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Franklin is a limited partner who spends 450 hours on partnership activities, while another limited partner, Graham, spends 520 hours. Both partners also each spend 200 hours on unrelated employment. How does the 500-hour threshold affect the classification of each partner''s income differently, and what is the consequence for their ability to use partnership losses?',
  choices = '["Both are treated as materially participating because limited partners are exempt from passive activity rules", "Franklin is treated as materially participating with 450 hours while Graham is not, because limited partners must stay below 500 hours to qualify", "Neither can materially participate regardless of hours because limited partner status permanently classifies all income as passive", "Franklin does NOT materially participate (450 < 500 hours) so his income/losses are passive, while Graham DOES materially participate (520 > 500 hours) so her income/losses are nonpassive — this means Graham can deduct partnership losses against wages while Franklin cannot"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (D): Limited partners face a restrictive set of material participation tests — the most accessible being the 500-hour test. Graham''s 520 hours meets this threshold, converting her partnership activity from passive to nonpassive, which allows losses to offset active income like wages. Franklin''s 450 hours falls short, so his income remains passive and losses can only offset other passive income. This distinction has major practical consequences: two investors in the same partnership with similar involvement levels can face opposite tax treatment based on a 50-hour difference. (A) is wrong because limited partners are subject to passive activity rules and are presumed NOT to materially participate. (B) reverses the rule — more hours are needed to qualify, not fewer. (C) is wrong because limited partners can materially participate if they meet the 500-hour test; the status is not permanently passive.'
WHERE id = 549;

-- ID 550: Individual Taxation: Income — MAGI phase-out threshold analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Garcia and her spouse file jointly and actively manage a rental property generating $25,000 in losses. Their MAGI is $110,000. Garcia''s single colleague, Torres, also actively manages a rental property with $25,000 in losses and has MAGI of $110,000. How does the $25,000 rental loss allowance phase-out affect each taxpayer, and why does filing status not change the phase-out threshold?',
  choices = '["Garcia deducts $25,000 and Torres deducts $0, because joint filers have a higher phase-out threshold of $150,000", "Garcia deducts $20,000 and Torres deducts $25,000, because the phase-out threshold is lower for joint filers at $75,000", "Both deduct $0 because the phase-out eliminates the entire allowance once MAGI exceeds $100,000 by any amount", "Both deduct $20,000 because the phase-out begins at $100,000 MAGI for ALL filing statuses — the $10,000 excess reduces the $25,000 allowance by $5,000 ($10,000 × 50%), and Congress set a uniform threshold to provide consistent treatment regardless of filing status"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (D): The $25,000 rental real estate loss allowance phase-out under Section 469(i) begins at $100,000 MAGI for all filing statuses (single, MFJ, head of household). This is unusual in the tax code — most phase-outs vary by filing status. Both Garcia and Torres have $10,000 of MAGI above the $100,000 threshold, which reduces the allowance by $5,000 ($10,000 × 50% rate), leaving $20,000 deductible for each. The uniform threshold means the allowance phases out identically for both. (A) is wrong because the phase-out threshold does not increase for joint filers. (B) is wrong because there is no lower threshold for joint filers — $100,000 applies to all. (C) is wrong because the phase-out is gradual ($1 reduction per $2 of excess MAGI), not a cliff that eliminates the entire allowance at $100,001.'
WHERE id = 550;

