-- ID 83: C Corporations — DRD ownership threshold analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Apex Industries owns 25% of Beacon Corp and receives $100,000 in dividends. Apex''s subsidiary, Crest Holdings, owns 85% of Delta Corp and also receives $100,000 in dividends. How does the ownership percentage affect the dividends received deduction (DRD) for each entity, and what policy goal does the tiered DRD structure serve?',
  choices = '["Apex deducts $50,000 (50%) and Crest deducts $100,000 (100%), because the tiers are set at below 20%, 20-79%, and 80%+", "Both deduct $65,000 (65%) because the DRD is a flat rate regardless of ownership percentage", "Apex deducts $100,000 (100%) and Crest deducts $65,000 (65%), because smaller ownership stakes receive larger deductions to encourage portfolio diversification", "Apex deducts $65,000 (65%) and Crest deducts $100,000 (100%), because the DRD increases with ownership level — at 80%+ ownership the full deduction eliminates triple taxation, while the 65% rate for 20-79% ownership provides partial relief from economic double taxation"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (D): The DRD uses three tiers based on ownership: below 20% gets 50%, 20-79% gets 65%, and 80%+ gets 100%. Apex''s 25% ownership falls in the middle tier ($100,000 × 65% = $65,000), while Crest''s 85% ownership qualifies for the full deduction ($100,000 × 100% = $100,000). The policy rationale is that higher ownership represents greater economic integration — at 80%+, the corporations are essentially a single economic unit, so full relief from multiple taxation is appropriate. Lower ownership represents more of a portfolio investment, warranting less relief. (A) is wrong about the tier breakpoints — the first tier is below 20%, not below 25%. (B) is wrong because the DRD is explicitly tiered, not flat. (C) reverses the relationship — larger ownership stakes receive larger deductions, not smaller ones.'
WHERE id = 83;

-- ID 231: C Corporations — Current vs. accumulated E&P interaction
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Orion Corp has current-year E&P of $100,000 and accumulated E&P of ($30,000) — a deficit. Orion distributes $90,000 to its sole shareholder, Burke. Burke''s tax advisor must determine the dividend treatment. If Orion instead had current E&P of $60,000 (with the same accumulated deficit of $30,000), how would the dividend characterization of the $90,000 distribution differ between the two scenarios?',
  choices = '["In the first scenario, the entire $90,000 is a dividend because current E&P of $100,000 is allocated first and exceeds the distribution; in the second scenario, only $30,000 is a dividend ($60,000 current minus $30,000 accumulated deficit), with $60,000 treated as return of capital — demonstrating that current E&P is tested before accumulated E&P", "In both scenarios, $70,000 is a dividend because current and accumulated E&P are always combined before testing", "In neither scenario is any portion a dividend because the accumulated deficit eliminates all E&P", "In both scenarios the full $90,000 is a dividend because distributions are always fully taxable regardless of E&P"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): The E&P ordering rules first allocate current E&P to distributions (pro rata if multiple distributions). In Scenario 1, current E&P of $100,000 exceeds the $90,000 distribution, making the entire amount a dividend — the accumulated deficit is never reached. In Scenario 2, current E&P of $60,000 covers only the first $60,000; the remaining $30,000 must be tested against accumulated E&P, which is a ($30,000) deficit, meaning no accumulated E&P supports the remaining distribution. Therefore, only $60,000 is a dividend (though technically the netting of $60,000 current against ($30,000) accumulated yields $30,000 of combined E&P, making $30,000 supported by net E&P). The key analytical insight is that current E&P is tested independently first, and the accumulated deficit reduces only the excess. (B) is wrong because current and accumulated E&P are not simply combined — current E&P has priority. (C) is wrong because an accumulated deficit does not eliminate current E&P. (D) is wrong because distributions in excess of E&P are return of capital, not dividends.'
WHERE id = 231;

-- ID 233: C Corporations — Section 351 basis preservation analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Whitaker transfers property with a basis of $50,000 and FMV of $80,000 to a newly formed corporation for 100% of the stock under Section 351. Whitaker''s attorney notes that the stock basis will be $50,000, not $80,000. What is the effect of Section 351''s basis rule on Whitaker''s future tax liability, and how would the outcome differ if the transaction did not qualify under Section 351?',
  choices = '["Under Section 351, Whitaker takes a $50,000 carryover basis, preserving $30,000 of built-in gain that will be recognized when the stock is sold — this is the trade-off for deferring the $30,000 gain at contribution; without Section 351, Whitaker would recognize $30,000 gain immediately but take an $80,000 FMV basis in the stock, eliminating future built-in gain", "Under Section 351, Whitaker takes an $80,000 FMV basis and no gain is ever recognized, while without Section 351, Whitaker recognizes $30,000 gain and takes a $50,000 basis", "Under both Section 351 and non-351 transfers, Whitaker takes a $50,000 basis because corporate formation rules always use carryover basis", "Under Section 351, Whitaker takes a $0 basis because nonrecognition transactions reset basis to zero"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): Section 351 provides nonrecognition on the transfer but preserves the $30,000 of built-in gain through a carryover basis of $50,000 in the stock. When Whitaker eventually sells the stock, the gain that was deferred at contribution will be recognized. If the transaction did not qualify under Section 351 (e.g., Whitaker received less than 80% control), the $30,000 gain ($80,000 FMV − $50,000 basis) would be recognized immediately, and the stock basis would be $80,000 (the FMV paid in a taxable exchange). The analytical distinction is that Section 351 is a timing difference, not a permanent exclusion — the gain is deferred, not forgiven. (B) reverses the basis outcomes — Section 351 uses carryover basis, not FMV. (C) is wrong because non-351 transfers are taxable exchanges where basis equals FMV. (D) is wrong because nonrecognition does not mean zero basis — it means the existing basis carries over.'
WHERE id = 233;

-- ID 570: C Corporations — Post-TCJA NOL limitation impact analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Pinnacle Corp has a post-2017 NOL carryforward of $500,000 and current-year taxable income (before NOL deduction) of $400,000. Its competitor, Summit Corp, has a pre-2018 NOL carryforward of $500,000 and the same $400,000 of current-year taxable income. How does the TCJA''s 80% limitation affect each company''s NOL deduction and remaining taxable income differently?',
  choices = '["Both companies deduct the full $400,000, eliminating taxable income, because the 80% limit applies only to NOLs exceeding $1 million", "Pinnacle deducts $320,000 (80% of $400,000) leaving $80,000 taxable, while Summit deducts the full $400,000 leaving $0 taxable — because pre-2018 NOLs are grandfathered and not subject to the 80% limitation, creating different tax outcomes from NOLs of different vintages", "Pinnacle deducts $400,000 while Summit deducts $320,000, because older NOLs receive less favorable treatment", "Both companies deduct $320,000 because the 80% limitation applies to all NOLs regardless of when they arose"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): The TCJA''s 80% limitation applies only to NOLs arising in tax years beginning after December 31, 2017. Pinnacle''s post-2017 NOL is limited to 80% of taxable income ($400,000 × 80% = $320,000), leaving $80,000 subject to tax. Summit''s pre-2018 NOL is grandfathered under the prior rules, which allowed a 100% offset — Summit deducts the full $400,000 and owes no tax. This vintage distinction means two companies with identical current income and identical NOL amounts can have vastly different tax liabilities depending solely on when the loss arose. (A) is wrong because there is no $1 million threshold for the 80% limitation. (C) reverses which vintage is more favorable — older NOLs actually receive better treatment. (D) is wrong because the TCJA explicitly grandfathered pre-2018 NOLs at 100%.'
WHERE id = 570;

-- ID 574: C Corporations — UBTI exclusion analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'A tax-exempt university operates two activities: a commercial bookstore selling to the general public and an investment portfolio generating dividends, interest, and royalties. The bookstore produces $200,000 of net income and the portfolio produces $150,000. The university''s CFO asks why one activity generates Unrelated Business Taxable Income (UBTI) while the other does not. What distinguishes the two, and what is the policy rationale for the exclusion?',
  choices = '["The bookstore income is excluded from UBTI because educational institutions are fully exempt, while the investment income is UBTI because it represents passive activity", "Both activities generate UBTI because any income-producing activity by a tax-exempt organization is unrelated business income", "The investment income ($150,000) is UBTI while the bookstore income ($200,000) is excluded, because commercial operations by universities are always considered related to their educational mission", "The bookstore income ($200,000) is UBTI because it constitutes a regularly carried-on trade or business not substantially related to the exempt purpose, while dividends, interest, and royalties ($150,000) are specifically excluded from UBTI — the exclusion exists because passive investment income does not create unfair competition with taxable businesses in the way active commercial operations do"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (D): UBTI targets income from active commercial operations that compete unfairly with taxable businesses. The bookstore selling to the general public is a regularly carried-on trade or business not substantially related to the university''s educational mission, generating UBTI. Dividends, interest, and royalties are specifically excluded from UBTI under Section 512(b) because passive investment returns do not involve the competitive advantage that tax-exempt status provides in active operations — any taxpayer can earn investment returns regardless of tax-exempt status. (A) is wrong because educational institutions are not fully exempt from UBTI — unrelated commercial activities are taxable. (B) is wrong because the Code specifically excludes certain categories of passive income. (C) reverses the treatment and incorrectly assumes all commercial university operations are related to education.'
WHERE id = 574;

-- ID 575: C Corporations — AET vs. PHC tax distinction analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Kendrick Holdings is a closely held C corporation that retains $5 million in earnings beyond its reasonable business needs, while Ashford Investments is a closely held C corporation where 85% of its income comes from dividends, interest, and royalties. Both companies face potential penalty taxes. How do the accumulated earnings tax (AET) and personal holding company (PHC) tax differ in what triggers each penalty, and why does the Code impose two separate penalty taxes?',
  choices = '["The AET applies only to public companies while the PHC tax applies only to private companies, and both exist to increase corporate tax revenue", "The AET targets Kendrick''s excessive accumulation of earnings beyond reasonable business needs, while the PHC tax targets Ashford''s concentration of passive investment income — the Code imposes two separate penalties because corporations can be used to defer shareholder-level tax through either retaining earnings or sheltering passive income, and each abuse requires a different triggering mechanism", "Both taxes apply identically to any corporation with retained earnings above $250,000, with the only difference being the tax rate", "The AET applies at a 75% rate to retained earnings while the PHC tax applies at 20% to passive income, and both can apply to S corporations"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): The AET and PHC tax address two distinct forms of tax avoidance using C corporations. The AET targets corporations like Kendrick that accumulate earnings beyond reasonable business needs to avoid distributing dividends (which would be taxable to shareholders). The PHC tax targets corporations like Ashford that serve as holding companies to shelter passive investment income from individual tax rates. Each tax has different triggering criteria because the underlying abuse is different: the AET looks at the purpose of retention, while the PHC tax looks at the composition of income and ownership concentration. Both impose a 20% penalty tax rate. (A) is wrong because the AET can apply to any C corporation, not just public ones. (C) is wrong because the triggers are fundamentally different — the AET examines business purpose while the PHC test examines income type and ownership. (D) is wrong about the AET rate (it is 20%, not 75%) and wrong that either applies to S corporations.'
WHERE id = 575;

