-- ID 89: Partnerships — Nonliquidating distribution basis ordering analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Partner Xavier has an outside basis of $60,000. The partnership distributes $45,000 cash and property with an inside basis of $25,000 (FMV $30,000) in a nonliquidating distribution. Xavier''s partner, Yates, has the same $60,000 outside basis but receives only the property (inside basis $25,000, FMV $30,000) with no cash. How does the ordering rule affect the basis of the distributed property differently for each partner, and why?',
  choices = '["Xavier takes a $25,000 basis and Yates takes a $25,000 basis, because property always retains its inside basis regardless of other distributions", "Xavier takes a $30,000 FMV basis and Yates takes a $30,000 FMV basis, because distributed property is always marked to market", "Xavier takes a $0 basis because cash exhausted all available basis, while Yates takes a $25,000 basis because no cash was distributed", "Xavier takes a $15,000 basis because cash reduces outside basis first ($60,000 − $45,000 = $15,000 remaining), and property basis is limited to that remainder; Yates takes the full $25,000 carryover basis because without cash, the entire outside basis is available to absorb the property''s inside basis"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (D): In nonliquidating distributions, cash reduces the partner''s outside basis first, dollar for dollar. Xavier''s $60,000 basis is reduced by $45,000 cash to $15,000. The property''s inside basis ($25,000) exceeds Xavier''s remaining outside basis ($15,000), so the property takes a $15,000 basis — the lesser amount. Yates receives no cash, so the full $60,000 outside basis is available; the property''s $25,000 inside basis fits entirely within Yates''s basis, and the property retains its $25,000 carryover basis. The ordering rule (cash first, then property) exists because cash has a fixed basis, while property basis can flex to prevent negative outside basis. (A) is wrong because property does not always retain inside basis — it can be reduced when the partner''s remaining outside basis is insufficient. (B) is wrong because distributed property is not marked to market. (C) is wrong because Xavier retains $15,000 after cash, not $0.'
WHERE id = 89;

-- ID 90: Partnerships — Separately stated items analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'A three-equal-partner partnership reports $300,000 of ordinary business income, $15,000 of long-term capital gains, and $9,000 of charitable contributions. Partner Avery asks why her K-1 shows $100,000 of ordinary income rather than $102,000 (one-third of $306,000 total income net of contributions). How does the separate statement requirement affect the character of income reported to each partner, and what would happen if these items were combined?',
  choices = '["Each partner reports $100,000 of ordinary income because long-term capital gains and charitable contributions must be separately stated to preserve their character at the partner level — if combined, capital gains would lose their preferential tax rate and charitable contributions would lose their deduction limitations, both of which vary by individual partner circumstances", "Each partner reports $102,000 of ordinary income because all partnership items are combined before allocation to simplify reporting", "Each partner reports $92,000 of ordinary income because capital gains and charitable contributions reduce the ordinary income allocation", "Each partner reports $108,000 of ordinary income because separately stated items are added back to ordinary income on the K-1"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): The partnership reports $100,000 of ordinary business income per partner ($300,000 ÷ 3). The $15,000 of long-term capital gains ($5,000 per partner) and $9,000 of charitable contributions ($3,000 per partner) are separately stated because each has special tax treatment that depends on the individual partner''s circumstances. Long-term capital gains are taxed at preferential rates that vary by the partner''s income bracket. Charitable contributions are subject to AGI-based percentage limitations that differ for each partner. If combined into ordinary income, these character-dependent rules could not be properly applied at the individual level. This is the fundamental mechanism of partnership pass-through taxation: the entity computes items but individual tax consequences are determined at the partner level. (B) is wrong because combining items would destroy character distinctions. (C) is wrong because separately stated items do not reduce ordinary income — they are simply reported on separate lines. (D) is wrong because separately stated items are not added back to ordinary income.'
WHERE id = 90;

-- ID 237: Partnerships — Section 721 nonrecognition rationale analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Palmer contributes equipment with a basis of $40,000 and FMV of $90,000 to form a partnership. Quinn contributes cash of $90,000. Both receive 50% interests. Palmer asks why she does not recognize the $50,000 built-in gain at contribution. What is the effect of Section 721 on Palmer''s unrealized gain, and how does the Code ensure this gain is not permanently excluded?',
  choices = '["Section 721 permanently excludes the $50,000 gain because contributions to partnerships are never taxed, and the Code does not have a mechanism to recapture it", "Section 721 defers the gain by requiring Palmer to take a $40,000 carryover basis in her partnership interest, while the partnership takes a $40,000 carryover basis in the equipment — these dual basis rules preserve the $50,000 built-in gain at both levels, ensuring it is recognized when Palmer sells her interest or the partnership sells the equipment", "Section 721 allows Palmer to recognize the gain immediately at capital gains rates as a tax benefit for new business formation", "Section 721 shifts the gain to Quinn because Quinn contributed cash equal to the equipment''s FMV, making Quinn responsible for the built-in gain"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Section 721 provides nonrecognition at contribution, but the gain is deferred, not excluded. Two basis mechanisms preserve the $50,000 built-in gain: (1) Palmer''s outside basis in her partnership interest is $40,000 (her carryover basis), so selling her 50% interest for $90,000 FMV would produce a $50,000 gain; and (2) the partnership''s inside basis in the equipment is $40,000, so selling the equipment at $90,000 FMV would produce $50,000 of gain allocated to Palmer under Section 704(c). These dual mechanisms ensure the gain is eventually recognized regardless of which disposal path occurs. (A) is wrong because the gain is deferred, not permanently excluded — the carryover basis ensures eventual recognition. (C) is wrong because Section 721 prevents gain recognition, it does not allow it. (D) is wrong because Section 704(c) specifically allocates built-in gain to the contributing partner, not other partners.'
WHERE id = 237;

-- ID 239: Partnerships — Section 704(c) built-in loss allocation analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Lawson contributes land with a basis of $200,000 and FMV of $120,000 to a partnership with two other equal partners. The land has a built-in loss of $80,000. Two years later, the partnership sells the land for $110,000. How does Section 704(c) affect the allocation of the $90,000 total loss ($200,000 basis − $110,000 sale price) among the partners, and what would happen without this rule?',
  choices = '["The $90,000 loss is split equally ($30,000 each) because partnership income and loss must always be allocated equally among equal partners", "Lawson bears the entire $90,000 loss because Section 704(c) assigns all losses on contributed property to the contributing partner regardless of amount", "The $90,000 loss is allocated $0 to Lawson and $45,000 each to the other two partners, because contributing partners cannot claim losses on property they contributed", "Lawson is allocated the $80,000 built-in loss (the decline that occurred before contribution) plus one-third of the $10,000 post-contribution decline, while the other partners each receive one-third of the $10,000 — without Section 704(c), the $80,000 pre-contribution loss would be split equally, shifting $53,333 of Lawson''s economic loss to the other partners"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (D): Section 704(c) requires that the $80,000 built-in loss (the difference between basis and FMV at contribution) be allocated entirely to Lawson because this loss existed before the other partners joined. Only the $10,000 post-contribution decline ($120,000 FMV at contribution − $110,000 sale price) is shared equally among all three partners ($3,333 each). Without Section 704(c), the entire $90,000 loss would be split equally ($30,000 each), effectively shifting $53,333 of Lawson''s pre-existing economic loss to the other two partners — a result Congress found inequitable. Section 704(c) ensures that economic consequences follow the partner who bore the risk. (A) is wrong because equal allocation ignores the pre-contribution built-in loss. (B) is wrong because Section 704(c) only assigns the built-in portion to the contributor; post-contribution changes are shared. (C) is wrong because it excludes Lawson from loss allocation entirely.'
WHERE id = 239;

-- ID 240: Partnerships — Guaranteed payment classification analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Partner Chen manages daily operations for Thornfield Partners LLP and receives a fixed $120,000 annual payment regardless of partnership profit. Partner Webb also performs management services but receives 30% of net income (which totaled $100,000, giving Webb $30,000). How does the classification of these two payments differ under IRC Section 707(c), and what is the effect on each partner''s self-employment tax treatment?',
  choices = '["Chen''s $120,000 is a guaranteed payment (determined without regard to partnership income) and is subject to self-employment tax, while Webb''s $30,000 is a distributive share of income (determined by reference to income) — both are taxable, but the guaranteed payment is deductible by the partnership while the distributive share is not, creating different effects on other partners'' allocations", "Both payments are guaranteed payments because both are compensation for services rendered to the partnership", "Chen''s payment is a distribution from profits and Webb''s is a guaranteed payment, because fixed amounts cannot be guaranteed payments", "Neither payment is a guaranteed payment because guaranteed payments can only be made for capital contributions, not services"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): The key distinction under Section 707(c) is whether the payment is determined "without regard to the income of the partnership." Chen''s fixed $120,000 meets this test — it is paid regardless of profits, making it a guaranteed payment. Webb''s 30% of net income is contingent on partnership income, making it a distributive share allocation. Both are included in the recipient''s ordinary income and subject to self-employment tax, but they differ in partnership-level treatment: the guaranteed payment to Chen is deducted by the partnership before computing remaining income to allocate to all partners (reducing other partners'' shares), while Webb''s distributive share is simply an allocation of already-computed income. (B) is wrong because the test is not about compensation for services but about whether the payment varies with income. (C) reverses the classification. (D) is wrong because guaranteed payments apply to both services and capital under Section 707(c).'
WHERE id = 240;

