-- ID 73: Property Transactions: Gains/Losses — Section 1245 recapture analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Hoffman sells business equipment for $80,000. The equipment was purchased for $100,000 and has $35,000 of accumulated depreciation (adjusted basis $65,000). Hoffman''s partner, Klein, sells a commercial building for $500,000 that cost $600,000 and has $150,000 of accumulated straight-line depreciation (adjusted basis $450,000). How does the depreciation recapture treatment differ between Section 1245 (equipment) and Section 1250 (real property), and what is the effect on each taxpayer''s gain character?',
  choices = '["Hoffman has $15,000 of ordinary income under Section 1245 because all gain up to depreciation taken is recaptured as ordinary income; Klein has $50,000 of gain taxed at a maximum 25% rate as unrecaptured Section 1250 gain because real property with straight-line depreciation is not subject to full ordinary recapture — this distinction exists because Congress viewed accelerated depreciation on personal property as a greater abuse potential", "Both have Section 1231 gains because any asset held over one year in a business qualifies for capital gain treatment regardless of depreciation", "Hoffman has $15,000 of long-term capital gain and Klein has $50,000 of ordinary income, because real property receives harsher recapture treatment than personal property", "Both have ordinary income equal to their total depreciation taken ($35,000 and $150,000 respectively), regardless of the actual gain on sale"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): Section 1245 requires full recapture of depreciation as ordinary income to the extent of gain. Hoffman''s $15,000 gain ($80,000 − $65,000) is entirely within the $35,000 of depreciation taken, so all $15,000 is ordinary income. Section 1250 treats real property differently: for straight-line depreciation on real property, the gain attributable to depreciation ($50,000 of Klein''s gain) is taxed as "unrecaptured Section 1250 gain" at a maximum 25% rate — not full ordinary income rates. Congress distinguished the two because personal property (Section 1245) often used accelerated depreciation that could exceed actual economic decline, creating a greater potential for converting ordinary deductions into capital gains. Real property with straight-line depreciation was considered less abusive. (B) is wrong because depreciation recapture overrides Section 1231 treatment. (C) reverses the relative harshness — personal property faces full ordinary recapture. (D) is wrong because recapture is limited to actual gain, not total depreciation taken.'
WHERE id = 73;

-- ID 215: Property Transactions: Gains/Losses — Holding period character analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Medina sells two blocks of identical stock: Block A was held for 8 months and sold at a $10,000 gain, while Block B was held for 14 months and sold at a $10,000 gain. Both gains are $10,000, but Medina''s CPA advises that the tax consequences differ significantly. How does the holding period affect the character and tax rate applied to each gain, and what is the practical impact on Medina''s total tax?',
  choices = '["Both gains are taxed identically because the holding period distinction was eliminated by the TCJA", "Block A''s gain is taxed at Medina''s ordinary income rate and Block B is also taxed at ordinary rates, but Block B qualifies for a $3,000 exclusion", "Block A''s gain is a long-term capital gain (favorable rate) and Block B''s is a short-term capital gain (ordinary rate), because shorter holding periods receive preferential treatment", "Block A''s $10,000 gain is short-term (held one year or less) and taxed at Medina''s ordinary income rate, while Block B''s $10,000 gain is long-term (held more than one year) and taxed at preferential rates of 0%, 15%, or 20% — for a taxpayer in the 32% ordinary bracket, this holding period difference on identical $10,000 gains could mean paying $3,200 vs. $1,500 in tax"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (D): The one-year holding period is the dividing line between short-term and long-term capital gains. Block A, held for 8 months, produces a short-term capital gain taxed at ordinary income rates (up to 37%). Block B, held for 14 months, produces a long-term capital gain taxed at preferential rates (0%, 15%, or 20% depending on income). For a taxpayer in the 32% bracket, the same $10,000 gain results in roughly $3,200 tax (short-term) versus $1,500 tax (long-term at 15%), a $1,700 difference from holding the stock 6 months longer. This preferential rate for long-term gains incentivizes longer-term investment. (A) is wrong because the TCJA preserved the LTCG preferential rates. (B) is wrong because there is no $3,000 exclusion for long-term gains — the $3,000 figure relates to capital loss deductions. (C) reverses the treatment — longer holding periods receive the preferential rate.'
WHERE id = 215;

-- ID 216: Property Transactions: Gains/Losses — Capital loss netting and limitation analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Wagner has net short-term capital losses of $8,000 and net long-term capital gains of $5,000. Wagner''s colleague, Fischer, has net short-term capital losses of $8,000 and net long-term capital gains of $0. How does the capital loss netting process produce different deduction amounts and carryforward consequences for each taxpayer?',
  choices = '["Wagner has a $3,000 net capital loss and deducts $3,000 against ordinary income with $0 carryforward; Fischer has an $8,000 net capital loss, deducts $3,000 against ordinary income, and carries forward $5,000 — the $3,000 annual limit applies to NET capital losses after gains and losses are offset, meaning Wagner''s $5,000 of long-term gains absorb most of the short-term losses before the limit applies", "Both deduct $8,000 against ordinary income because there is no annual limitation on capital loss deductions", "Wagner deducts $3,000 with a $5,000 carryforward and Fischer deducts $3,000 with a $5,000 carryforward — both have identical outcomes because gains and losses are not netted", "Wagner deducts $0 because gains and losses cancel out, while Fischer deducts $8,000 in full"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): Capital gains and losses must first be netted against each other. Wagner''s $8,000 short-term loss is offset by $5,000 of long-term gains, leaving a $3,000 net capital loss — exactly the maximum deductible amount ($3,000 limit), with nothing to carry forward. Fischer has $8,000 of short-term losses with no gains to offset, producing an $8,000 net capital loss. The $3,000 annual limitation allows $3,000 to be deducted against ordinary income, with $5,000 carried forward to future years. The netting process is crucial because it determines how much of the $3,000 limit is consumed. (B) is wrong because the $3,000 annual limit on net capital losses has existed since 1978. (C) is wrong because gains and losses must be netted before applying the limitation — without netting, Wagner''s outcome is calculated incorrectly. (D) is wrong because Wagner''s losses exceed gains by $3,000, producing a deductible loss, not $0.'
WHERE id = 216;

-- ID 217: Property Transactions: Gains/Losses — Section 1250 unrecaptured gain analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Patterson sells a commercial building (depreciable real property) held for 20 years with $300,000 of accumulated straight-line depreciation, producing a $400,000 total gain. In the same year, Patterson also sells manufacturing equipment (Section 1245 personal property) with $100,000 of accumulated depreciation, producing an $80,000 gain. How does the recapture treatment differ between these two asset types, and what effect does the "unrecaptured Section 1250 gain" category have on Patterson''s tax rate?',
  choices = '["Both assets face identical treatment — all gains attributable to depreciation are taxed at ordinary income rates for any depreciable property", "The building gain is fully taxed as long-term capital gain at 15%/20% rates because Section 1250 does not apply to straight-line depreciation, while the equipment''s $80,000 gain is ordinary under Section 1245", "The building''s $300,000 attributable to depreciation is taxed at a maximum 25% rate as unrecaptured Section 1250 gain with the remaining $100,000 at 15%/20% LTCG rates, while the equipment''s entire $80,000 gain is recaptured as ordinary income under Section 1245 — this creates three different tax rates on Patterson''s total gains from two asset sales", "The building and equipment gains are combined and taxed at a blended rate of 22% because mixed asset sales require a unified rate calculation"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (C): Patterson faces three distinct tax rates. The building''s $300,000 of gain attributable to straight-line depreciation is "unrecaptured Section 1250 gain" taxed at a maximum 25% rate — a special category between ordinary rates and LTCG rates. The remaining $100,000 of building gain (appreciation beyond cost) is taxed at standard LTCG rates (15% or 20%). The equipment''s entire $80,000 gain is taxed as ordinary income under Section 1245''s full recapture rule. Section 1250''s 25% rate for unrecaptured gain on real property is a compromise: it is higher than LTCG rates (acknowledging that depreciation deductions reduced ordinary income) but lower than full ordinary rates (recognizing that straight-line depreciation on real property approximates actual economic depreciation). (A) is wrong because real property with straight-line depreciation does not face full ordinary recapture. (B) is wrong because Section 1250 does apply — it imposes the 25% rate, not 15%/20%. (D) is wrong because there is no blended rate concept for mixed asset sales.'
WHERE id = 217;

-- ID 522: Property Transactions: Gains/Losses — NIIT application analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Harrison, a single filer, has MAGI of $280,000, which includes $60,000 of net investment income (NII). His colleague, Blake, also single, has MAGI of $220,000 with $100,000 of NII. The NIIT threshold for single filers is $200,000. How does the "lesser of" calculation under IRC Section 1411 produce different NIIT amounts for each taxpayer despite Blake having more net investment income?',
  choices = '["Harrison pays $2,280 (3.8% × $60,000 lesser amount) and Blake pays $760 (3.8% × $20,000 lesser amount), because the NIIT is calculated on the lesser of NII or MAGI excess over the threshold — Harrison''s larger NII drives a higher tax even though Blake has more NII in absolute terms", "Both pay 3.8% on their full NII ($2,280 and $3,800 respectively) because the NIIT applies to all net investment income once MAGI exceeds the threshold", "Harrison pays $3,040 (3.8% × $80,000 MAGI excess) and Blake pays $3,800 (3.8% × $100,000 NII), because the NIIT always uses the larger of NII or MAGI excess", "Neither pays NIIT because the 3.8% surtax applies only to taxpayers with MAGI above $400,000"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): The NIIT under Section 1411 is 3.8% of the lesser of (1) net investment income or (2) MAGI exceeding the threshold. Harrison: MAGI excess = $80,000 ($280,000 − $200,000), NII = $60,000, lesser = $60,000 × 3.8% = $2,280. Blake: MAGI excess = $20,000 ($220,000 − $200,000), NII = $100,000, lesser = $20,000 × 3.8% = $760. The "lesser of" mechanism means Blake''s $100,000 of NII is largely irrelevant because only $20,000 of MAGI exceeds the threshold. Harrison pays more ($2,280 vs. $760) despite having less NII because the MAGI excess component is larger. This dual-variable calculation prevents the surtax from applying disproportionately to investment income earned by taxpayers who barely exceed the threshold. (B) is wrong because the NIIT does not apply to all NII — only the lesser of the two measures. (C) is wrong because the calculation uses the lesser, not the greater. (D) is wrong because the single-filer threshold is $200,000, not $400,000.'
WHERE id = 522;

