-- LIKE-KIND EXCHANGES (5 questions)
-- ============================================================

-- ID 75: Like-Kind Exchanges — Boot recognition analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Callahan exchanges an investment property (adjusted basis $200,000, FMV $350,000) for like-kind property (FMV $300,000) plus $50,000 cash. Callahan''s neighbor, Drake, completes a similar exchange but receives like-kind property (FMV $350,000) with no cash. How does the receipt of boot affect Callahan''s gain recognition compared to Drake''s, and what is the policy rationale for this treatment?',
  choices = '["Both recognize $0 gain because Section 1031 provides complete nonrecognition for any exchange of like-kind property, regardless of whether cash is involved", "Callahan recognizes $50,000 gain (limited to boot received) while Drake recognizes $0 — boot triggers gain recognition because the taxpayer has received liquid value that is not reinvested in like-kind property, breaking the continuity of investment that justifies deferral", "Callahan recognizes $150,000 (full realized gain) while Drake recognizes $0, because receiving any boot requires full gain recognition", "Callahan recognizes $50,000 and Drake recognizes $150,000, because pure exchanges without boot receive harsher treatment"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): In a Section 1031 exchange, gain is recognized to the extent of boot (non-like-kind property or cash) received, but never exceeds the realized gain. Callahan''s realized gain is $150,000 ($350,000 FMV − $200,000 basis), but recognition is limited to the $50,000 of boot received. Drake receives no boot, so no gain is recognized. The policy rationale: Section 1031 defers gain when the taxpayer maintains a continuous investment in like-kind property. The $50,000 cash Callahan receives represents a partial cashing out — those funds are no longer invested in real property, so deferral is inappropriate for that portion. Drake''s pure exchange maintains full continuity. (A) is wrong because boot breaks the complete deferral. (C) is wrong because boot caps recognition at the boot amount, not the full realized gain. (D) reverses the treatment — pure exchanges receive the most favorable treatment.'
WHERE id = 75;

-- ID 219: Like-Kind Exchanges — Boot classification analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Rodriguez exchanges a commercial warehouse (adjusted basis $280,000, FMV $410,000) for an office building ($375,000 FMV) plus $35,000 cash through a qualified intermediary. Separately, Rodriguez also assumes a $20,000 liability on the office building. Rodriguez''s CPA must determine what constitutes "boot" in this transaction. How does the cash received and the liability assumed interact to determine total boot, and why does boot classification matter for gain recognition?',
  choices = '["Only the $35,000 cash is boot because liabilities assumed by the taxpayer never constitute boot — Rodriguez recognizes $35,000 of gain", "The $35,000 cash is boot (triggers gain), while the $20,000 liability assumed by Rodriguez is NOT boot — it reduces total boot because assuming a liability is the opposite of receiving value; Rodriguez recognizes $35,000 of gain limited by the lesser of boot received or realized gain", "Both the $35,000 cash and the $20,000 liability are boot, totaling $55,000 of recognized gain", "Neither the cash nor the liability constitutes boot when a qualified intermediary is involved, so Rodriguez recognizes $0 gain"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Boot in a Section 1031 exchange is non-like-kind property or cash that the taxpayer receives, triggering gain recognition. The $35,000 cash is boot because it represents value extracted from the exchange. However, the $20,000 liability Rodriguez assumes on the replacement property is not boot — assuming a liability going in is economically equivalent to paying additional consideration, which reduces rather than increases the taxpayer''s cash position. Boot classification matters because gain is recognized only to the extent of net boot received (but never exceeding realized gain). If Rodriguez had been relieved of a liability on the relinquished property, that relief would be treated as boot received. The distinction is directional: debt relief = boot received; debt assumption = additional investment. (A) reaches the right gain amount but fails to explain the liability interaction. (C) is wrong because liabilities assumed by the taxpayer are not boot — they represent additional investment. (D) is wrong because QI involvement does not eliminate boot classification for cash received.'
WHERE id = 219;

-- ID 220: Like-Kind Exchanges — Identification and closing deadline analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Patel transfers relinquished property on March 1 in a deferred Section 1031 exchange. Patel identifies three replacement properties by April 14 but cannot close on any of them by the 45-day deadline. Patel''s CPA suggests identifying a fourth property on April 20. Meanwhile, Patel''s colleague, Sharma, transferred property on the same date and identified one replacement by April 10, closing on July 15. How do the two exchange timelines compare, and what is the consequence of Patel''s late identification?',
  choices = '["Patel can identify the fourth property on April 20 because the 45-day rule allows modifications until closing, and Sharma''s exchange qualifies because closing occurred within 180 days", "Both exchanges fail because the IRS requires identification and closing within 45 days total", "Patel''s attempt to identify a fourth property on April 20 fails because the 45-day identification deadline (April 15) has passed — any identification after that date is invalid and the exchange fails if no timely-identified property is acquired; Sharma''s exchange succeeds because identification occurred within 45 days (April 10) and closing within 180 days (July 15 = 136 days)", "Patel''s exchange succeeds with a 10% penalty for late identification, while Sharma''s exchange is standard"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (C): Deferred Section 1031 exchanges impose two strict deadlines from the transfer of the relinquished property: (1) the replacement must be identified within 45 days, and (2) closing must occur within 180 days (or the tax return due date, whichever is earlier). Patel''s March 1 transfer sets the 45-day deadline at April 15. The April 20 identification attempt is 5 days late and is completely invalid — there is no extension, modification, or penalty option. If Patel cannot acquire one of the three timely-identified properties, the exchange fails entirely, and the transaction is treated as a taxable sale. Sharma met both deadlines: identified by April 10 (40 days) and closed by July 15 (136 days). These strict deadlines prevent taxpayers from using open-ended exchanges as indefinite deferral vehicles. (A) is wrong because the 45-day deadline is absolute with no modification period. (B) is wrong because closing has a separate 180-day window. (D) is wrong because there is no penalty option — late identification simply invalidates the exchange.'
WHERE id = 220;

-- ID 221: Like-Kind Exchanges — Mortgage relief as boot analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Evans exchanges an office building (adjusted basis $300,000, FMV $500,000, subject to a $100,000 mortgage) for a warehouse (FMV $400,000) with no mortgage. The buyer assumes Evans''s $100,000 mortgage. Evans''s CPA explains that debt relief is treated as boot. How does the mortgage assumption affect Evans''s gain recognition, and how would the result change if Evans had instead assumed a $60,000 mortgage on the warehouse?',
  choices = '["Evans recognizes $100,000 because net debt relief of $100,000 is boot; if Evans assumed a $60,000 mortgage on the warehouse, net debt relief would be $40,000 ($100,000 relieved − $60,000 assumed), reducing recognized gain to $40,000 — this netting principle treats mortgage relief and assumption as a single computation because both represent changes in the taxpayer''s economic investment position", "Evans recognizes $200,000 (full realized gain) because any mortgage involvement disqualifies the exchange from Section 1031", "Evans recognizes $0 because mortgage assumptions between parties are not treated as boot in like-kind exchanges", "Evans recognizes $100,000 in both scenarios because liabilities assumed on the replacement property do not reduce boot"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): When a taxpayer is relieved of a mortgage in a Section 1031 exchange, the relief is treated as boot received (equivalent to receiving cash). Evans''s $100,000 mortgage relief is boot, and the recognized gain is $100,000 (limited by the realized gain of $200,000). If Evans also assumed a $60,000 mortgage on the warehouse, liabilities are netted: $100,000 relief − $60,000 assumed = $40,000 net boot, reducing recognized gain to $40,000. The netting principle reflects economic reality — Evans''s true cash-equivalent extraction from the exchange is the net change in indebtedness. (B) is wrong because mortgage involvement does not disqualify the exchange; it merely triggers partial gain recognition. (C) is wrong because mortgage relief has been treated as boot since the regulations were established. (D) is wrong because assumed liabilities on replacement property directly reduce net boot — this netting is fundamental to Section 1031 calculations.'
WHERE id = 221;

-- ID 530: Like-Kind Exchanges — Installment method gain recognition analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Mitchell sells investment land (basis $200,000) for $500,000, receiving $100,000 at closing and $100,000 per year for four additional years. The gross profit percentage is 60% ($300,000 gain ÷ $500,000 price). Mitchell''s colleague, O''Brien, sells identical land but receives the full $500,000 at closing. How does the installment method affect the timing and amount of Mitchell''s gain recognition compared to O''Brien''s, and what is the economic rationale?',
  choices = '["Mitchell defers all $300,000 of gain to the final payment year, while O''Brien recognizes $300,000 immediately — the installment method stacks gain at the end to maximize deferral", "Mitchell recognizes $60,000 of gain per year (60% gross profit ratio × $100,000 payment) over five years, while O''Brien recognizes $300,000 entirely in the year of sale — the installment method matches gain recognition to cash flow, preventing taxpayers from owing tax on gain they have not yet received in cash", "Both recognize $300,000 in the year of sale because the installment method was repealed for sales above $250,000", "Mitchell recognizes $300,000 in year one and O''Brien spreads recognition over five years, because the installment method applies only to lump-sum sales"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Under IRC Section 453, the installment method recognizes gain proportionally as payments are received. Mitchell''s 60% gross profit percentage means each $100,000 payment includes $60,000 of gain and $40,000 of basis recovery. Over five years, Mitchell recognizes $60,000 per year, totaling $300,000 — the same total gain as O''Brien, but spread over time. O''Brien receives all cash at closing and recognizes the full $300,000 immediately. The economic rationale is that taxpayers should not be required to pay tax on gain before receiving the cash to pay it. Without the installment method, Mitchell would owe tax on $300,000 in year one despite receiving only $100,000. (A) is wrong because gain is not deferred to the final year — it is recognized proportionally with each payment. (C) is wrong because the installment method remains available for most non-dealer sales. (D) reverses the application — the installment method applies to deferred-payment sales, not lump-sum sales.'
WHERE id = 530;

