-- ID 86: S Corporations — Basis adjustment ordering analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Shareholder Archer owns 40% of an S corporation that reports $200,000 of ordinary business income. Archer''s beginning stock basis is $50,000, and the corporation distributes $100,000 to Archer during the year. Archer''s fellow shareholder, Bates (also 40%), has a beginning basis of $20,000 and receives the same $100,000 distribution. How does the ordering of basis adjustments (income first, then distributions) produce different tax consequences for Archer versus Bates?',
  choices = '["Both end with $30,000 basis and no taxable gain because S corporation distributions are never taxable", "Archer ends with $30,000 basis and no gain ($50,000 + $80,000 income − $100,000 distribution), while Bates ends with $0 basis and no gain because income increases basis before distributions are tested — without the income-first rule, Bates''s $100,000 distribution would have exceeded the $20,000 beginning basis by $80,000, potentially creating taxable gain", "Archer ends with $30,000 basis and Bates recognizes $80,000 of taxable gain because the distribution exceeds beginning basis", "Both recognize $20,000 of capital gain because distributions that exceed 50% of income are taxable"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): S corporation basis adjustments follow a specific ordering: income items increase basis first, then distributions reduce basis. Archer: $50,000 beginning + $80,000 income share (40% × $200,000) = $130,000, then − $100,000 distribution = $30,000 ending basis. Bates: $20,000 beginning + $80,000 income share = $100,000, then − $100,000 distribution = $0 ending basis. Without the income-first ordering rule, Bates''s $100,000 distribution would be tested against only $20,000 of basis, producing $80,000 of taxable gain — an inequitable result since Bates already has $80,000 of income allocated. The ordering rule prevents double taxation by ensuring that current income increases basis before distributions are measured. (A) is wrong because distributions exceeding basis are taxable as capital gain. (C) is wrong because income increases basis before the distribution is tested. (D) is wrong — there is no 50%-of-income test for distribution taxation.'
WHERE id = 86;

-- ID 87: S Corporations — Loss limitation basis comparison analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Shareholder Crawford has $30,000 stock basis and a $20,000 direct loan to the S corporation. Shareholder Dawson has $30,000 stock basis and personally guaranteed a $20,000 corporate bank loan. Both are allocated $45,000 of S corporation losses. How does the basis limitation treat the two shareholders differently, and how does this compare to partnership loss limitation rules?',
  choices = '["Crawford deducts $45,000 and Dawson deducts $45,000, because both direct loans and guarantees provide equal basis for loss deduction purposes in S corporations and partnerships", "Crawford deducts $30,000 (stock only) and Dawson deducts $50,000 (stock + guarantee), because guarantees provide more basis than direct loans", "Crawford deducts $45,000 (stock $30,000 + loan $20,000 = $50,000 exceeds $45,000 loss) while Dawson deducts only $30,000 (stock basis only, guarantee creates no basis) — this S corporation rule differs from partnerships, where certain entity-level debt can increase a partner''s basis through liability sharing rules", "Both deduct $30,000 because neither loans nor guarantees create additional basis in S corporations"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (C): In S corporations, debt basis for loss deduction requires a direct economic outlay from the shareholder to the corporation. Crawford''s $20,000 direct loan creates debt basis, giving total basis of $50,000 ($30,000 stock + $20,000 loan), sufficient to deduct the entire $45,000 loss. Dawson''s personal guarantee of a bank loan does not create debt basis because no funds flowed directly from Dawson to the corporation — the loan is between the bank and the corporation, with Dawson merely a guarantor. Dawson''s deduction is limited to $30,000 stock basis, with $15,000 suspended. This is a key distinction from partnerships: under Section 752, partners can increase basis through allocable shares of partnership liabilities (including recourse and nonrecourse debt), even without a direct outlay. (A) is wrong because guarantees and direct loans are not equivalent in S corporations. (B) reverses the treatment. (D) is wrong because direct loans do create S corporation debt basis.'
WHERE id = 87;

-- ID 234: S Corporations — Shareholder eligibility analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Bennett, a U.S. citizen, and her business partners — Tanaka (a nonresident alien), Vertex Partners LLC (a partnership), and Harper Family Trust (an eligible domestic trust) — each want to acquire shares in an S corporation. How do the shareholder eligibility rules affect each potential shareholder''s ability to participate, and what is the consequence if an ineligible shareholder acquires stock?',
  choices = '["All four can be shareholders because S corporation eligibility rules were eliminated by the TCJA", "Bennett and Harper Family Trust are eligible shareholders, but Tanaka and Vertex Partners are not — Tanaka''s nonresident alien status and Vertex''s entity type (partnership) are both disqualifying; if either acquires stock, the S election terminates retroactively to the date of the disqualifying transfer, converting the corporation to C corporation status", "Only Bennett is eligible because trusts, nonresident aliens, and partnerships are all ineligible S corporation shareholders", "Bennett, Tanaka, and Harper Family Trust are eligible, but Vertex Partners is not — nonresident aliens were made eligible by recent legislation"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): S corporation shareholder eligibility under Section 1361(b) is strictly limited. Eligible shareholders include U.S. citizen and resident individuals (Bennett qualifies), certain domestic trusts like qualifying subchapter S trusts and electing small business trusts (Harper Family Trust qualifies if it meets the requirements). Ineligible shareholders include nonresident aliens (Tanaka is disqualified — only U.S. citizens and residents qualify) and partnerships, LLCs taxed as partnerships, and C corporations (Vertex Partners is disqualified). The consequence of an ineligible shareholder acquiring stock is severe: the S election terminates, and the corporation becomes a C corporation, subjecting all income to double taxation. This makes shareholder monitoring critical for S corporations. (A) is wrong because the TCJA did not eliminate eligibility restrictions. (C) is wrong because certain trusts are eligible. (D) is wrong because nonresident aliens remain ineligible.'
WHERE id = 234;

-- ID 236: S Corporations — Required tax year analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Whitfield Technology Inc. previously operated as a C corporation with a September 30 fiscal year-end. Upon electing S corporation status effective January 1, 2025, Whitfield asks to maintain the September 30 year-end. The CPA explains that the tax year must change. Why does the Code require S corporations to use a calendar year, and what is the effect of this requirement on income deferral?',
  choices = '["S corporations must use a calendar year (December 31) unless a valid business purpose is established, because individual shareholders report on a calendar year — allowing a September 30 fiscal year would create a built-in deferral, as income earned from October through December would not be reported on the shareholder''s return until the following calendar year, giving shareholders an automatic 3-15 month deferral advantage not available to other taxpayers", "S corporations can use any fiscal year because the pass-through rules automatically match income to the shareholder''s tax year regardless of entity year-end", "S corporations must use a September 30 fiscal year to align with quarterly estimated tax payments", "S corporations must match the majority shareholder''s fiscal year, which may be any 12-month period the shareholder elects"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): IRC Section 1378 requires S corporations to use a calendar year unless the corporation establishes a valid business purpose for a different year-end (a difficult standard to meet) or elects under Section 444 with a required payment. The rationale is anti-deferral: individual shareholders file on a calendar year, so allowing a September 30 fiscal year would mean income earned October 1 through December 31 would not appear on the shareholder''s K-1 until the following year — up to a 15-month deferral for income earned in October. This systemic deferral advantage was considered unfair compared to self-employed individuals and partners in calendar-year partnerships. (B) is wrong because fiscal years create real timing mismatches — the pass-through rules do not automatically solve this. (C) is wrong because there is no September 30 requirement, and estimated payments are not the basis for the rule. (D) is wrong because S corporations default to calendar year, not the majority shareholder''s year.'
WHERE id = 236;

