-- ID 246: Estate and Gift Tax — Annual exclusion application analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'For 2026, Henderson gives $25,000 to each of four friends. Henderson''s spouse does not consent to gift splitting. The annual exclusion is $19,000 per donee. How does the annual exclusion affect Henderson''s total taxable gifts, and what would be the effect if Henderson''s spouse consented to gift splitting on all four gifts?',
  choices = '["Henderson has $100,000 in taxable gifts without splitting and $0 with splitting, because gift splitting doubles the exclusion per donee", "Henderson has $24,000 in taxable gifts without splitting ($6,000 excess × 4 donees) and $0 with splitting ($19,000 × 2 spouses = $38,000 exclusion per donee exceeds $25,000 gift) — gift splitting effectively treats each gift as made half by each spouse, doubling the per-donee exclusion from $19,000 to $38,000 and eliminating the taxable portion entirely", "Henderson has $0 taxable gifts in both scenarios because the annual exclusion applies per donor without limit", "Henderson has $6,000 in taxable gifts without splitting and $3,000 with splitting, because the exclusion applies to total gifts not per donee"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Without gift splitting, Henderson''s $25,000 gift to each friend exceeds the $19,000 annual exclusion by $6,000 per donee, creating $24,000 in total taxable gifts ($6,000 × 4 donees) that reduce Henderson''s lifetime exemption. With gift splitting under Section 2513, each $25,000 gift is treated as if Henderson gave $12,500 and the spouse gave $12,500. Since both amounts are below the $19,000 per-donee exclusion, no gift is taxable — zero lifetime exemption is used. The $24,000 difference demonstrates the significant planning value of gift splitting for married couples: it doubles the per-donee exclusion from $19,000 to $38,000 without the spouse needing separate assets. (A) is wrong about the without-splitting amount ($100,000 would be the total gifts, not the taxable amount). (C) is wrong because the per-donee exclusion is $19,000, not unlimited. (D) is wrong because the exclusion is per donee, not a single aggregate limit.'
WHERE id = 246;

-- ID 247: Estate and Gift Tax — Gift tax exclusion classification analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'In 2025, Davidson makes four transfers: $50,000 cash to a friend Collins, $25,000 to a qualified charity, $18,000 tuition paid directly to a grandchild''s university, and $12,000 of medical bills paid directly to a hospital for a child. Davidson''s CPA must determine which transfers are subject to gift tax. What distinguishes the taxable transfer from the excluded transfers, and why does the Code treat direct educational and medical payments differently from cash gifts?',
  choices = '["All four transfers are subject to gift tax because the Code does not distinguish between types of donees", "Only the charitable donation is subject to gift tax because gifts to charities above $25,000 trigger the gift tax", "Only the $50,000 to Collins is subject to gift tax (to the extent it exceeds the $19,000 annual exclusion), because the charitable deduction offsets the charity gift, while tuition and medical payments made directly to the institution are completely excluded under Section 2503(e) — the direct-payment requirement exists to ensure funds are actually used for qualifying purposes rather than providing the recipient with unrestricted cash that could be spent on anything", "The tuition and medical payments are subject to gift tax while the cash gift and charitable donation are not, because transfers to institutions are always taxable"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (C): The gift tax applies to transfers of property for less than full consideration, but several exclusions and deductions exist. The $50,000 to Collins is subject to gift tax (less the $19,000 annual exclusion, so $31,000 is taxable). The $25,000 charitable donation qualifies for the gift tax charitable deduction, eliminating any gift tax. The $18,000 tuition and $12,000 medical bills qualify for the Section 2503(e) unlimited exclusion for direct educational and medical payments. The critical requirement is "direct to the institution" — if Davidson had given the $18,000 to the grandchild to pay tuition, it would be a regular gift subject to the annual exclusion limit. Congress created the direct-payment requirement to target the exclusion at genuine education and healthcare expenses rather than providing a loophole for unlimited tax-free cash transfers. (A) is wrong because the Code explicitly distinguishes between transfer types. (B) is wrong because charitable gifts receive a deduction, not taxation. (D) reverses the treatment of every category.'
WHERE id = 247;

-- ID 248: Estate and Gift Tax — Gross estate inclusion analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'A decedent owned the following at death: a solely-owned home ($500,000), a life insurance policy with a $1,000,000 death benefit payable to a named beneficiary, a jointly held brokerage account with a spouse ($400,000 total, 50% includable), and a revocable trust holding $300,000. The decedent''s will distributes only the home. The executor asks why assets not passing through the will are included in the gross estate. How does the estate tax concept of "gross estate" differ from the probate estate, and what is the consequence for total estate tax liability?',
  choices = '["Only the $500,000 home is in the gross estate because it is the only asset in the will — non-probate assets like life insurance, joint accounts, and trusts are excluded from estate tax", "The gross estate includes only probate assets ($500,000) plus jointly held assets ($200,000) = $700,000, because life insurance and revocable trusts are always excluded", "The gross estate includes all assets in which the decedent had an interest or control at death: the home ($500,000), life insurance ($1,000,000 — included because the decedent held incidents of ownership), joint account ($200,000 — the decedent''s 50% share), and revocable trust ($300,000 — included because the power to revoke equals ownership) = $2,000,000 total, far exceeding the $500,000 probate estate — this broader inclusion prevents estate tax avoidance through non-probate transfer mechanisms", "All four assets are included at their combined value of $2,200,000 because joint accounts are always fully included"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (C): The gross estate under Sections 2031-2044 is far broader than the probate estate. It captures all assets in which the decedent had ownership, control, or incidents of ownership at death, regardless of how they transfer. The home ($500,000) is included as solely-owned property. Life insurance ($1,000,000) is included under Section 2042 when the decedent held incidents of ownership (policy ownership, right to change beneficiaries). The joint account contributes $200,000 (50% under Section 2040 for spousal joint tenancy). The revocable trust ($300,000) is included under Section 2038 because the power to revoke is equivalent to ownership. The total $2,000,000 gross estate dwarfs the $500,000 probate estate. This broad definition prevents taxpayers from avoiding estate tax simply by titling assets in non-probate forms. (A) is wrong because the gross estate extends far beyond the will. (B) excludes life insurance and revocable trusts, which are explicitly included. (D) is wrong about the joint account — spousal joint tenancy includes only 50%, not 100%.'
WHERE id = 248;

-- ID 564: Estate and Gift Tax — Gift splitting benefit analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'A married couple, the Nguyens, want to give $100,000 to their grandchild in 2026 (annual exclusion: $19,000). Without gift splitting, the entire gift comes from one spouse. With gift splitting, each spouse is treated as giving $50,000. How does gift splitting affect the amount of lifetime exemption each spouse must use, and why might this matter for estate planning purposes?',
  choices = '["Without splitting, one spouse uses $81,000 of lifetime exemption ($100,000 − $19,000); with splitting, each spouse uses $31,000 ($50,000 − $19,000 each) for a combined $62,000 — splitting saves $19,000 of total lifetime exemption because it applies two annual exclusions instead of one, preserving more exemption for transfers at death when estate tax rates apply", "Without splitting, no lifetime exemption is used because the annual exclusion covers the full $100,000", "With splitting, each spouse uses $50,000 of exemption because the annual exclusion does not apply to split gifts", "Gift splitting has no effect on lifetime exemption usage — it only changes which spouse reports the gift on Form 709"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): Without gift splitting, one spouse makes the full $100,000 gift, using one $19,000 annual exclusion and consuming $81,000 of that spouse''s lifetime exemption. With gift splitting under Section 2513, the gift is treated as $50,000 from each spouse. Each spouse applies a $19,000 annual exclusion, creating only $31,000 of taxable gift per spouse ($62,000 combined). The $19,000 savings in lifetime exemption ($81,000 − $62,000) matters because lifetime exemption used for gifts reduces the exemption available at death. At the 40% estate tax rate, $19,000 of preserved exemption could save $7,600 in estate taxes. Over years of gifting, the cumulative savings from applying two exclusions instead of one can be substantial. (B) is wrong because $19,000 covers only a portion of $100,000. (C) is wrong because the annual exclusion applies to split gifts — that is the primary benefit. (D) is wrong because splitting fundamentally changes exemption usage by applying two exclusions.'
WHERE id = 564;

-- ID 565: Estate and Gift Tax — GSTT purpose and mechanism analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Thornton, a wealthy grandparent, plans two transfer strategies: Strategy A transfers $2 million directly to a grandchild (skipping the child generation), while Strategy B transfers $2 million to the child, who later transfers it to the grandchild. Thornton''s estate planner warns that Strategy A may trigger the generation-skipping transfer tax (GSTT) in addition to gift tax. How does the GSTT prevent a tax advantage that Strategy A would otherwise provide over Strategy B, and what economic consequence does it impose?',
  choices = '["Strategy A incurs only gift tax while Strategy B incurs transfer tax twice (parent-to-child and child-to-grandchild) — the GSTT eliminates this advantage by imposing a flat-rate tax on Strategy A equal to the highest estate tax rate (40%), ensuring that skipping a generation does not reduce total transfer taxes compared to sequential transfers through each generation", "Strategy A avoids all transfer taxes because direct gifts to grandchildren are exempt under the GSTT exclusion", "The GSTT applies to Strategy B, not Strategy A, because it penalizes transfers that pass through multiple generations", "Both strategies face identical tax treatment because the GSTT was repealed by the TCJA"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): Without the GSTT, Strategy A (direct transfer to grandchild) would bypass one level of transfer taxation. In Strategy B, the $2 million is taxed when Thornton transfers it to the child (gift or estate tax), and taxed again when the child transfers it to the grandchild — two taxable events. Strategy A would incur only one taxable event (Thornton to grandchild), saving potentially hundreds of thousands in transfer taxes. The GSTT prevents this by imposing an additional tax at the highest estate tax rate (40%) on generation-skipping transfers, making the total tax burden approximately equivalent to sequential transfers through each generation. The GSTT has its own exemption (matching the estate tax exemption), so transfers within the exemption amount are not affected. (B) is wrong because while a GSTT exemption exists, transfers exceeding it are taxed at 40%. (C) reverses which strategy triggers the GSTT. (D) is wrong because the GSTT was not repealed by the TCJA — it remains in effect.'
WHERE id = 565;

COMMIT;